<http://data.silknow.org/object/75f443a1-dcf1-3968-9eda-d491028f9f14/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "This embroidered picture depicts the Buddha Maitreya (the Buddha of the Future), who awaits his time to appear to deliver all sentient beings from suffering.\n\nMaitreya is posed with the Dharmachakra Mudra (hand gesture), which is a gesture of teaching, and also interpreted as turning the Wheel of Dharma (the Law).\n\nDuring the 18th century the Qing court was devoted to Buddhism, which took the form of Tibetan Lamaism, as religious links with Tibet were strong. An inscription on the back written in Chinese, Tibetan, Manchu and Mongol (four of the principal languages of the Qing Dynasty, 1644 - 1911) indicates that this scroll was an imperial gift to a Tibetan dignitary by the name of Ngag-dbang dPalrbyor."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/75f443a1-dcf1-3968-9eda-d491028f9f14> .

<http://data.silknow.org/production/9d6b86d2-3690-3808-bdc6-b089d09d63db>
